cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file."
title: "OES-Resilience: Benchmarking telemetry intelligence under real-world stress"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Brisson"
    given-names: "Jean-François"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-9778-5374"
    affiliation: "Spark AI NLP"
version: 0.4.0
date-released: "2026-09-30"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23071166
license: AGPL-3.0-only
repository-code: "https://github.com/sparkainlp-x/oes-resilience"
url: "https://sparkainlpx.xyz"
abstract: >-
  Open, reproducible benchmark for multichannel telemetry anomaly detection.
  Synthetic 512-channel signals are split into sixteen 32-channel blocks and
  scored by block-level detectors behind a plugin API: the transparent OES32
  reference detector (0.45 max|x| + 0.35 RMS + 0.20 mean|x|), a robust
  z-score baseline, EWMA and CUSUM temporal detectors, an oes32+ewma hybrid
  and an optional Isolation Forest, compared in a calibrated scorecard.
  Version 0.4.0 adds replay evaluation: a JSONL telemetry replay is scored
  against a hashed preregistration, with every detector's threshold calibrated
  by default to the same ~1% false-positive budget on a hash-locked,
  event-free calibration replay (fixed thresholds carry a fairness note).
  Version 0.3.0 added a seeded stress suite (heavy-tailed noise, missing
  channels, saturation and clipping, narrow and cross-block events, global
  shift, correlated blocks, drift and baseline steps) with robustness metrics
  (FP inflation, recall retention, Wilson intervals), explicit mask-aware
  handling of missing data, and honest negative results. Synthetic data only;
  not quantum, not GPS, not a physical sensor, and no performance claims about
  real systems.
keywords:
  - anomaly-detection
  - benchmark
  - synthetic-data
  - telemetry
  - numpy
  - reproducibility
  - observability
  - operational-resilience
  - signal-processing
  - scientific-software
